        h: 'Not only to return — to return in peace',
        p: 'Parshat Vayetzei is about exile and redemption: Yaakov leaves the Land of Israel for Charan, to Lavan, and at the end of the parshah he is saved from him and goes on his way to the Land of Israel. As he leaves, he asks not only to come back whole, but that the return itself be “in peace,” in calm. And so it was: Lavan chased him with bad intentions, yet everything passed in peace, without war. This is the parshah’s tie to 10 Kislev: the liberation of the Mitteler Rebbe is connected to the verse “He has redeemed my soul in peace” — not only redemption from the enemy, but redemption by way of peace. With the Alter Rebbe, on 19 Kislev, this verse came into his reading only because the day was a Tuesday; on 10 Kislev it is part of the Tehillim portion for that day of the month — everyone says it, every year.',
